  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Cough for about three months, blood-streaked sputum for two weeks, night sweats, and weight loss of about 6 kg in a man of 28, with a right upper lobe cavity on chest X-ray and CT: suspected pulmonary tuberculosis; home airborne isolation and three sputum samples for acid-fast smear, nucleic acid amplification, and culture</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R04.2</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem linked to his documented chronic cough and hemoptysis; superseded by the diagnosis at the second visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Pulmonary tuberculosis of the right upper lobe, cavitary and sputum smear-positive, confirmed by nucleic acid amplification without rifampin resistance</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>A15.0</td><td>S — Synthetic modern coding of his documented tuberculosis, chosen over the disputed genetic and cardiac alternatives; the cavity and all bacteriology are synthetic.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Moderate protein-calorie malnutrition: BMI 16.8, about 11 percent weight loss in three months, and albumin 3.2 g/dL</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E44.0</td><td>S — Synthetic nutrition diagnosis anchored to his documented frailty and exhaustion.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Acute viral upper respiratory infection during the continuation phase of tuberculosis treatment; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J06.9</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent illness chosen to teach how to tell a cold from tuberculosis treatment failure, and moxifloxacin interactions with cold remedies; it does not echo a documented illness.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

