<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. A cold caught from his students: a short illness with a sore throat and runny nose before the cough, a normal chest examination, and continued weight gain. This is not tuberculosis treatment failure, which would bring back cough with sputum, night sweats, weight loss, or hemoptysis over weeks, and his April cultures were negative. Antibiotics would not help. Zinc lozenges and mineral-containing cold remedies bind moxifloxacin and lower its absorption, and an antibiotic such as azithromycin would add to the QT effect of moxifloxacin. [S]<list><item>Fluids, honey, rest, and saline nose spray; acetaminophen for throat pain or fever.</item><item>No zinc lozenges or mineral-containing remedies within four hours before or eight hours after his morning dose; no antibiotic.</item><item>Keep taking every dose on the video calls.</item><item>Wear a mask while coughing and move lessons online for a few days, as anyone with a cold would.</item><item>Return for a cough lasting beyond two to three weeks, blood in the sputum, night sweats, or weight loss, for sputum smear and culture.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pulmonary tuberculosis, continuation phase (A15.0)</content>. Responding well: symptoms of tuberculosis gone, weight rising, every dose observed. No tendon pain, tingling, or signs of liver injury. [S]<list><item>Continue rifapentine, moxifloxacin, isoniazid, and pyridoxine to the 119th dose.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); Interim Guidance: 4-Month Rifapentine-Moxifloxacin Regimen for the Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Pulmonary Tuberculosis - United States, 2022 (2022); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Message if the cold is not better in a week. End-of-treatment visit after the last dose in July. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-21: end-of-treatment review after 119 doses. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
